<body>
    <h1>AI-338:  VLM Service</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p></p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-338:VLMService-VideoAnalysisPipeline">Video Analysis Pipeline<=
/h1>
<p>Here's a look at the architecture for our video processing pipeline. Aft=
er a good bit of experimentation, the implementation ends up being more eff=
icient, and specifically tailored to the way modern Vision Language Models =
(VLMs) work. The core philosophy is to treat the VLM's inference step as th=
e most expensive part of the entire process (because it is, by an order of =
magnitude) and optimize everything around making that step as efficient as =
possible.</p>
<p>The overall workflow can be broken down into two main stages: a pre-flig=
ht<strong> </strong>stage where we gather and organize all our data, and an=
 inference stage where we interact with the model.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">[Video =
File] -&gt; [Stage 1: Pre-Flight] -&gt; [Stage 2: Execution] -&gt; [Final R=
esults]
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This design might seem a little counterintuitive at first. One might thi=
nk that a streaming pipeline, where we process frames as soon as they're av=
ailable, would be faster. However, testing and analysis showed that the cos=
t of a single VLM inference call (around two minutes in testing) is so high=
 that it's far better to spend a little extra time upfront (about 40 second=
s) to prepare perfectly optimized batches. Consider the following real-worl=
d metrics of performance on an NVIDIA L40S card:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>VLM Inference Time:</strong> 120 seconds</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Total Frame Extraction Time:</strong> 40 seconds</p></li>
</ul>
<p>An extra, non-optimally packed inference call would cost 120 seconds. Th=
e <em>total</em> time saved by overlapping the entire 40-second extraction =
process is, at most, 40 seconds. Therefore, any workflow that risks even on=
e extra inference call is a net loss.</p>
<p>Thus the current implementation, which prioritizes creating the absolute=
 minimum number of batches, is the correct one because the number of VLM in=
ference calls is the single most important metric to minimize.<br></p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-338:VLMService-Pre-Flight">Pre-Flight</h2>
<p>This first stage is all about gathering the necessary ingredients and or=
ganizing them for the main event. It ensures we don't send the VLM too much=
, too little, or poorly organized data.</p>
<p></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">       =
                +---------------------------+      +-----------------------=
---+
[Video File] --------&gt; | 1. Scene Detection        | ---&gt; | 2. Dynami=
c Frame Sampling|
                       | (PySceneDetect w/ scaling)|      | (Calculate fram=
e counts) |
                       +---------------------------+      +----------------=
----------+
                                                                     |
                                                                     V
                       +---------------------------+      +----------------=
----------+
| 4. Batch Planning         | &lt;--- | 3. Bulk Frame Extraction   |
| (The "Pre-Flight Check")  |      | (Optimized OpenCV pass)  |
                       +---------------------------+      +----------------=
----------+
                               |
                               V
                     [Optimized Batch Plan]
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Scene Detection:</strong> We start by running the video through =
PySceneDetect. This gives us natural, context-aware boundaries for analysis=
. To make this step faster without sacrificing much accuracy, we employ a d=
ynamic downscaling algorithm. It automatically calculates a downscale facto=
r to bring any source video (4K, 1080p, etc.) to a consistent, smaller reso=
lution like 360p for analysis. This is much faster and more robust than har=
dcoding rules for specific resolutions.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Dynamic Frame Sampling:</strong> Once we have our scenes, we dec=
ide how many frames we actually need from each one. We've eschewed a simple=
, fixed frames-per-second approach. Instead, we use a dynamic algorithm tha=
t calculates the target number of frames based on the scene's duration, bou=
nded by a configurable minimum and maximum. A short 6-second scene might ge=
t 12 frames, while a long 30-second scene gets 60. This prevents us from se=
nding the VLM excess data for a secne; we=E2=80=99re building an appropriat=
e amount of context for the length of each scene</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Bulk Frame Extraction:</strong> With a master list of every sing=
le timestamp we need from the entire video, we make one pass using our hard=
ware-accelerated OpenCV extractor. Its smart-seeking logic minimizes expens=
ive seek operations by only seeking across large time gaps and reading sequ=
entially over small ones. This is vastly more efficient than seeking for ea=
ch and every frame individually.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>The "Pre-Flight Check" (Batch Planning):</strong> This is the br=
ains of the operation. We take the full list of prepared scenes and run the=
m through a greedy packing algorithm. The only rule is to pack as many scen=
es as possible into a single batch without letting the <em>total frame coun=
t</em> of that batch exceed a hard computational limit (which would cause C=
UDA OOM errors). This algorithm naturally allows for mixing a large scene w=
ith a few small scenes, ensuring no space is wasted. This step produces a f=
inal, immutable "batch plan."<br><br>
NOTE: Any scene that is too large on its own is skipped and flagged. Later,=
 we might want to build heuristics to fit the number of frames into a proce=
ssable batch.</p></li>
</ol>
<h2 id=3D"AI-338:VLMService-Inference">Inference</h2>
<p>With the plan in hand, the execution stage is clean and predictable. We =
are no longer making decisions on the fly; we are simply executing the opti=
mized plan.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">       =
                                               +------------------+
[Optimized Batch Plan] ---&gt; [ For each batch... ] ---&gt; | VLM Inferenc=
e    |
 (List of Batches)                                    | (Batch a, b, c)  |
                                                      +------------------+
                                                              |
                                                              V
                                                      +------------------+
                                                      | Reassembly       |
                                                      | (Using indices)  |
                                                      +------------------+
                                                              |
                                                              V
                                                        [Final Results]
                                                         (in order)
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The key here is that we've attached the original <code>scene_index</code=
> to our data from the very beginning. This index travels with the scene da=
ta through every step, allowing us to correctly reassemble the final result=
s in their original order, even though the scenes were processed in reorgan=
ized, optimized batches.</p>
<p>For the inference step itself, we can leverage model-level optimizations=
. Techniques like <strong>Flash Attention 2</strong> and <strong>quantizati=
on</strong> are employed directly on the model to attack the two-minute inf=
erence time, as this provides a greater performance dividend than any furth=
er pipeline tweaks.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
